unifolium, < L. ''unus'', one, + ''folium'', leaf.] A former genus of plants, of the order ''Liliaceæ'', including ''Smilacina'' and ''Maianthemum''.

'''uniforate''' (ū-ni-fō′rāt), ''a.'' [< L. ''unus'', one, + ''foratus'', pp. of ''forare'', bore, pierce: see ''foramen''.] Having one opening, pore, or foramen.

'''uniform''' (ū′ni-fôrm), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [I. ''a.'' F. ''uniforme'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''uniforme'', < L. ''uniformis'', having only one shape or form, < ''unus'', one, + ''forma'', form, shape. Cf. ''biform'', ''triform'', ''multiform''. II. ''n.'' = D. G. Sw. Dan. ''uniform'', < F. ''uniforme'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''uniforme'', uniform dress; from the adj.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Having always the same form; not changing in shape, appearance, character, etc.; in general, not variable; unchanging.
{{smaller block|All human bodies, for example, though each of them consists of almost an infinite number of parts, are perfectly ''uniform'' in their structure and functions.
{{right|''Beattie'', Moral Science, ii. 1.}}
The experience has been ''uniform'' that it is the gentle soul that makes the firm hero after all.
{{right|''Emerson'', Harvard Commemoration.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''a'') Not varying in degree or rate; equable; invariable: as, a ''uniform'' heat; a ''uniform'' motion (that is, the motion of a body when it passes over equal spaces in equal times).}}
{{smaller block|They [temperature observations] appear to go far to establish a nearly ''uniform'' temperature for abyssal depths, not far from the freezing-point of fresh water.
{{right|''C. Wyville Thomson'', Depths of the Sea, p. 359.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''b'') Having only one character throughout; homogeneous.}}
{{smaller block|Sometimes there are many parts of a law, and sometimes it is ''uniform'', and hath in it but one duty.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Rule of Conscience, iii. 6.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''c'') Consistent at all times; not different.}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
{{smaller block|If the Creator is perfect, his action must be ''uniform;'' anything else would be unworthy of him.
{{right|''Dawson'', Nature and the Bible, p. 31.}}}}
{{smaller|(''d'') Not different at different times or places: applicable to all places, or to all divisions of a country: as, a ''uniform'' tax; a ''uniform'' bankruptcy law. (''e'') Of the same appearance, pattern, or style.}}
{{smaller block|The practice of clothing soldiers by regiments in one ''uniform'' dress was not introduced by Louis XIV. till 1665, and did not become general in our army for many years afterward.
{{right|''Harper’s Mag.'', LXXX. 333.}}}}
'''2.''' Of the same form or character with others; agreeing with each other; conforming to one rule or mode.
{{smaller block|The only doubt is about the manner of their unity, how far churches are bound to be ''uniform'' in their ceremonies.
{{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Uniform acceleration.''' See ''acceleration'' (''b'').—'''Uniform current,''' a continuous current of constant strength.—'''Uniform extension, field, function, sandpiper†, symmetry,''' etc. See the nouns.—'''Uniform strain.''' Same as ''homogeneous strain'' (which see, under ''strain''<sup>1</sup>). ='''Syn.''' Unvarying, unchanging, alike, regular, constant, undeviating, consistent.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' A dress of the same kind, fabrics, fashion, or general appearance as others worn by the members of the same body, whether military, naval, or any other, by which the members may be recognized as belonging to the particular body: opposed to ''plain clothes'', or ordinary civil dress: as, the ''uniform'' of a soldier, a sailor, or a policeman.
{{smaller block|The ''uniforms'' in the army were plain and serviceable; the most picturesque being that of the Grenadiers, who, Evelyn says, were first introduced in 1678.
{{right|''Ashton'', Social Life in Reign of Queen Anne, II. 202.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|The proposed ''uniform'', sir, of the Pickwick Club.
{{right|''Dickens.''}}}}

¶
'''uniform''' (ū′ni-fôrm), ''v. t.'' [< ''uniform'', ''a.''] '''1.''' To make uniform; reduce to uniformity. ''Sir P. Sidney.''
{{smaller block|The more than Protean travesties which words underwent before they were ''uniformed'' by Johnson and Walker.
{{right|''Lowell'', Study Windows, p. 351.}}}}
'''2.''' To clothe with or as if with a uniform.
{{smaller block|This was the first flag bearing the state arms, and was carried by the first ''uniformed'' company of militia in the State [Michigan]. ''Preble'', Hist. Flag, p. 642.}}

'''uniformal†''' (ū-ni-fôr′mal), ''a.'' [< ''uniform'' + ''-al.''] Uniform; symmetrical.
{{smaller block|<poem>Her comlye nose with ''uniformall'' grace,
Like purest white, stands in the middle place.</poem>
{{right|''Herrick'', Appendix, p. 433.}}}}

'''uniformitarian''' (ū-ni-fôr-mi-tā′ri-an), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ''uniformit-y'' + ''-arian.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' Of or pertaining to uniformity or the doctrine of uniformity. See the noun.
{{smaller block|The catastrophist and the ''uniformitarian'' opinions.
{{right|''Whewell'', Hist. of Scientific Ideas, II. 289.}}}}
{{smaller block|The ''uniformitarian'' theories of Sir Charles Lyell were regarded as heresies by many.
{{right|''Westminster Rev.'', CXXVI. 544.}}}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' One who upholds a system or doctrine of uniformity; specifically, in ''geol.'', one who advocates the theory that causes now active in bringing about geological changes have always been similar in character and intensity, or,

readings.c3s1:
in other words, that there has been no essential change in the character of geological events during the lapse of the geological ages: the opposite of ''catastrophist''.
{{smaller block|The Catastrophist constructs Theories, the ''Uniformitarian'' demolishes them. The former adduces evidence of an Origin, the latter explains the evidence away.
{{right|''Whewell'', Philos. of Inductive Sciences, I., p. xxxvi.}}}}

'''uniformitarianism''' (ū-ni-fôr-mi-tā′ri-an-izm), ''n.'' [< ''uniformitarian'' + ''-ism''.] The theory advocated by uniformitarians: the opposite of ''catastrophism''. See ''catastrophe'', 3, and ''catastrophism''.
{{smaller block|The changes of the past must be investigated in the light of similar changes now in operation. This was the guiding principle of the Scottish School, . . . though under the name of ''Uniformitarianism'' it has unquestionably been pushed to an unwarrantable length by some of the later followers of Hutton.
{{right|''Geikie'', Geol. Sketches, p. 293.}}}}

'''uniformity''' (ū-ni-fôr′mi-ti), ''n.'' [= F. ''uniformité'' = Sp. ''uniformidad'' = Pg. ''uniformidade'' = It. ''uniformità'', < LL. ''uniformita(t-)s'', uniformity, < L. ''uniformis'', uniform: see ''uniform''.] The state or character of being uniform, in any sense; absence of variation or difference. {{smaller|(''a'') Maintenance of the same character, course, plan, laws, etc.; sameness; consistency.}}
{{smaller block|There is no ''uniformity'' in the design of Spenser; he aims at the accomplishment of no one action.
{{right|''Dryden.''}}

Queen Elizabeth was remarkable for that steadiness and ''uniformity'' which ran through all her actions.
{{right|''Addison.''}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|How far away is the doctrine of ''uniformity'' [in nature] from fatalism! It begins directly to remind us that men suffer from preventible evils, that the people perisheth for lack of knowledge. {{right|''W. K. Clifford'', Lect., II. 263.}}}}

{{smaller block|We see that only as fast as the practice of the arts develops the idea of measure can the consciousness of ''uniformity'' become clear.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Prin. of Psychol. (2d ed.), § 488.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') Conformity among several or many to one pattern, plan, rule, etc.; resemblance, consonance, or agreement: as, the ''uniformity'' of different churches in ceremonies or rites.}}

{{smaller block|Houses are built to live in, and not to look on; therefore let use be preferred before ''uniformity'', except where both may be had. {{right|''Bacon'', Building.}}}}

{{smaller block|Such is the ''uniformity'' of almost al the houses of the same streete . . . that they are made alike both in proportion of workmanship and matter.
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 30.}}}}

{{smaller block|The skilful campaign by which the triumph of the Reformation and of ''uniformity'' was secured.
{{right|''R. W. Dixon'', Hist. Church of Eng., xv.}}}}

{{smaller|(''c'') Continued or unvaried sameness or likeness; monotony.}}

{{smaller block|''Uniformity'' must tire at last, though it is a ''uniformity'' of excellence. {{right|''Johnson.''}}}}

{{smaller|'''Acts of Uniformity.''' See ''act.''}}

'''uniformize''' (ū′ni-fôrm-īz), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''uniformized'', ppr. ''uniformizing''. [< ''uniform'' + ''-ize''.] To make uniform; unify. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The other Congress expressed a similar wish for the formation of . . . an International Commission to fix units and ''uniformize'' methods. {{right|''Nature'', XL. 563.}}}}

'''uniformly''' (ū′ni-fôrm-li), ''adv.'' In a uniform manner; with uniformity; evenly; invariably.

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|In a light drab he ''uniformly'' dress’d.
{{right|''Crabbe'', Tales (Works, IV. 135).}}}}
{{smaller block|No assigned nor any conceivable attribute of the supposed archetypal vertebra is ''uniformly'' maintained.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Prin. of Biol. (Am. ed. 1872), § 210.}}}}
{{smaller block|When the simultaneous values of a quantity for different bodies or places are equal, the quantity is said to be ''uniformly'' distributed in space.
{{right|''Clerk Maxwell'', Matter and Motion, xxiii., foot-note.}}}}

'''Uniformly accelerated motion.''' {{smaller|See ''acceleration'' (''b'').}} —'''Uniformly retarded motion.''' {{smaller|See ''retard''.}}

'''uniformness''' (ū′ni-fôrm-nes), ''n.'' The state or character of being uniform; uniformity. ''Berkeley.''

'''unifoveate''' (ū-ni-fō′vē-āt), ''a.'' [< L. ''unus'', one, + ''fovea'', a small pit: see ''foveate''.] In ''entom.'', having a single fovea.

'''unify''' (ū′ni-fī), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''unified'', ppr. ''unifying''. [< F. ''unifier'' = Sp. ''unificar'' = It. ''unificare'', < ML. ''unificare'', make one, < L. ''unus'', one, + ''facere'', make: see ''-fy''. Cf. ''unific''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To form into one; make a unit of; reduce to unity or uniformity.
{{smaller block|Perception is thus a ''unifying'' act. {{right|''Sir W. Hamilton.''}}}}
{{smaller block|Unless we succeed in finding a rationale of this universal metamorphosis, we obviously fall short of that completely ''unified'' knowledge constituting philosophy.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', First Principles, p. 397.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To produce unity or uniformity.
{{smaller block|These Homeridæ were not the only authors of epic poems, but they had the great advantage over other epic bards that they were a genos, and that they worked continuously from generation to generation on the same poems, adding and ''unifying'', and so they produced the epics which have outlived all others.
{{right|''Classical Rev.'', II. 256.}}}}
